  {
    "episode_id": 88974,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/8/88974.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 42,
    "turn_text": "At the same time, I invited Congresswoman Chisholm as the first African-American woman elected to Congress to come speak to the Student Union. I had no idea she was running for president. And so after she spoke, I met her and talked with her. she spoke fluent Spanish. She talked about immigrant rights. She talked about reducing and eliminating poverty. She was a guest of Vietnam War. She was an educator, talked about childcare. Every issue that I cared about, she talked about in her speech. And I went up to her later. And I said, Ms. Chisholm, you know, I have this class, I'm about to flunk, and, you know, I'm supposed to wear a campaign, maybe I'll reconsider. So she took me to task, and she said to me, and she always called me even to her last month of life on this Earth, little girl, you have got to get on the inside and shake things up if you believe in what you're pushing and saying. She said, you've kind of registered a vote. I said, no way. I don't believe, just like many young people, I do not believe in this two-party system because the Democrats haven't done anything and the Republicans have. Why should I be party to that craziness that's irrelevant?",
