    "turn_type_label": "Substantive"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 88974,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/8/88974.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 44,
    "turn_text": "It's not, I see me and them. I mean, because I was exactly there. And it was really passing this class. I mean, I loved her and here is a black woman running for president and it may not got to do something. So I went back and she told me the registered vote, you've got to help me. I said, well, who do I call? How do I get involved? She said, I don't have a lot of national money and my local organizers who believe in me are doing something. So I went back to the class. I contacted some friends and we ended up organizing the Shirley Chisholm Presidential Northern California primary. Out of my class at Mills College, I got an A in the class and I went on to Miami as a Shirley Chisholm delegate and we took about 8% of the vote and out me to count. And Shirley Chisholm became a close friend and I traveled with her. I didn't work for her formally, but I was one of her key Northern California fundraisers and volunteers. And I got to know her really well. And then I went on to work for my beloved, Ron Dell. And I got a chance to be with Shirley Chisholm here in Washington DC while working for Ron. And I saw how she had to fight as a black woman just to get on the rules committee. They put her on the agriculture committee and she wanted to get on labor age. So she made a heck of a lot out of ag for urban gardens, you know, and for food security and nutrition. So I saw her take racism and sexism and turn it around and make it into something she wanted. And like she told me, she says, you can't go along to get along if you're a black woman. And she said, and you've got to get in and not go along with these rules. She said, they weren't made. for you, you've got to go in and change the rules of the game and shake things up. And that's what she did. Every step of the way, she pushed back. I mean, you have every member of Congress on her saying she was crazy, saying, why is Shirley doing this? She's pushing the envelope. She's not a team player. She's the she was very independent. I mean, I remember when she went down to see George Wallace when he was shot in the hospital and I was so upset, I said, I'm gonna leave this campaign, you know, the segregationist",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
